{
  "Subjective": "Patient reports staying at work until nine or ten o'clock three to four nights a week due to an end-of-month product launch deadline, describing the stress as \"swimming in it.\" She notes her chest gets tight around three in the afternoon daily. Sleep is poor; she falls asleep but wakes at four a.m. running through a list. Appetite has decreased as she skips lunch unintentionally. She reports increasing alcohol use to two or three glasses in the evening, up from one glass a couple times per week in June. She denies self-harm thoughts.",
  "Objective": "Patient presented fidgety, manipulating her ring throughout the session. Speech was fast, faster than usual, but not pressured. She was oriented and tracked content well. Affect was anxious but appropriate to the topic discussed. No acute indicators were noted. The patient directly denied self-harm thoughts.",
  "Assessment": "This appears to be a continuation of the established stress-reactivity pattern rather than a new episode. The product launch is the current trigger. While she has tools and uses some, such as box breathing, the increase in alcohol consumption is a concern to monitor. The four a.m. waking is more worrisome than the patient acknowledges. Functionally, she is holding it together and performing well at work.",
  "Plan": "Continue weekly sessions. Introduce a scheduled worry window of twenty minutes in the evening for two weeks; outside this window, she will write down and park worries. Patient agreed to try this despite initial skepticism. Revisit alcohol use at the next session. Primary care referral regarding physical symptoms remains undecided."
}

