{
  "Presenting problem": "Patient reports waking up with dread before any events occur in the day, occurring most days (five out of seven) over the past four months. She expresses worry about her job at a position she has held for eight years due to an ongoing reorganization and uncertainty about her role's survival. She describes checking her work email at 2:00 AM. Physical symptoms include jaw clenching and headaches occurring perhaps twice a week, which she reports are new.",
  "History": "Patient is in therapy for the first time with no prior psychiatric hospitalizations or current or past mental health medications. She reports her mother was a worrier but was never treated or diagnosed. She denies substance use concerns other than having had a drink or two on weekends, which has been steady for years. She is medically healthy, seeing her primary care doctor annually; the last visit was in spring with no issues. She is married for twelve years to one child (age seven). She denied any thoughts of self-harm or suicide, past or present, and appeared unbothered by the question.",
  "Formulation": "Patient presents with generalized anxiety over four-plus months that is functionally impairing, as sleep difficulties and morning dread are costing her. The reorganization is a plausible precipitant acting on top of what appears to be a longstanding anxious temperament and family loading. There is no evidence of mood disorder or disclosed trauma history, and no risk indicators were identified. Prognosis is considered good due to her motivation and psychological mindedness.",
  "Plan": "Begin weekly CBT sessions starting with psychoeducation and self-monitoring; patient was given a worry log to bring back. If sleep issues have not improved by six or eight weeks, discuss whether a medication consult is warranted, though this is not an immediate action. Next session scheduled for the same time next week."
}

